Z||000011||Seanh||11-05-2000||11:58 AM||||Wireing shouldnt be a big deal. Like I said before, the A terminal on the clip is negative, and B is positive. If you use a component set, you will run those wires into the crossover, and run wires from the crossover to the woofer and tweeter. They will be marked as to being positive or negative. I am a little confused as to where a good place to mount the crossover would be? Since I only put one 4" speaker in each stock hole, I mounted the crossovers on the underside of the wooden panel I made. This will work for you too, but you will have to make the panels with a little overhang at the end to mount it to.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">   <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>If you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||11-05-2000||03:04 PM||||Here is a pic of my install of the Polk Audio EX seriesII 4" with 1" tweeter.<BR>I put the crossover in the kick panel.<BR>Putting 2 4" speakers might require some trimming of the dash for the magnets. But there will be no room for the tweeter. I tried to see if it was possible. I planned on using just 1 4" on each side.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/speaker1.jpg">  <P>BTW ~ I still have a very nice set of Pioneer 4X10's for sale.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 11-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||fropuff||11-05-2000||05:27 PM||||Okay, I just got two sets of Pioneeer 4s.  I really like them.  They were on sale, too!  I do not, however, know how to wire these up.  I know that on the stock wiring, terminal A is -, B is +.  I also know where the + and - are on the speakers.  How do I make them work?  I don't want to screw anything up, even though I did get the 4-year service plan.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Philphine||11-05-2000||06:50 PM||||you can probaly wire them in parallel if you have an aftermarket stereo. probaly with a factory stereo too but i'm not as sure. it seemed like the factory speakers were 10 ohm and that would be a big impedance drop (about 2 ohms wired in parallel).<P>i have components in pods in the dead pedal area with my x-overs where the front speakers originaly were. it makes wiring easy if you hook into the factory radio and wiring and is convenient once you start to switch things out.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fropuff||11-05-2000||06:54 PM||||I'm keeping the stock stereo for the time being.  Each of the speakers are 4 ohms as well.  Thanks.<p>[This message has been edited by fropuff (edited 11-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Seanh||11-05-2000||07:01 PM||||I would be weary about useing the stock radio at 2 omhs, but it may work okay. If you are wondering what parallel is: Just get some speaker wire, wire the twospeakers together, positive to positive and negative to negative. Then hook the factory wireing to one of them (probally the closest one to the center of the car), positive to positive and negative to negative. That should do it for you. If you components, then use the same process I stated above, but treat the crossover as the speaker, and wire them together and hook the factory wires to one of them. Then just run speaker wire to the woofer and tweeter normally. Let me know if you have any problems.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">    <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>If you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I><p>[This message has been edited by Seanh (edited 11-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Philphine||11-05-2000||07:03 PM||||forgot. guess i should have told you parallel is wiring all the positives (+ from the radio and both speakers on their respective sides) together and do the same for the negatives. if you're worried about the impedance being too low and want to play it safe at first you can wire them in series. go from the positive wire from your stereo to the positive of one speaker. go from the negative of that speaker to the positive of the 2nd speaker. then wire from the negative of the 2nd speaker to the neg. wire from your stereo. series wiring it think will put you close to the factory stereo impedance. if you do have an aftermarket stereo parallel wiring should be more efficient (louder).||||reg||1
Z||000018||Formula||11-05-2000||09:14 PM||||series would be 8 ohms, and par. would be 2 ohms.<P>series is Rt= R1 + R2 ....... which is 8 ohms<P>parellel is Rt = (R1^-1+R2^-1)^-1..... which is 2 ohms<P>just thought I would share the formulas for those who dont know how to get the ohms<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||fropuff||11-05-2000||09:33 PM||||So I'm guessing that I will go on a series circuit.  That will be at 8 ohms, which is closer to the stock speakers than if I did a parallel circuit at 2.  When I do get a new stereo, I could switch to parallel.  Okay, I want to be sure I get this right:  <BR>1)+ on stereo    to + on speaker 1<BR>2)- on speaker 1 to + on speaker 2<BR>3)- on speaker 2 to - on stereo<BR>Is this right?  Thanks a lot.  I will wire them up tomorrow if I understand correctly.  Thanks again for all the help.  That should be it for now, unless I run into some problems. <P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.<p>[This message has been edited by fropuff (edited 11-05-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000020||Seanh||11-05-2000||10:28 PM||||Yes, that is correct for series. I would think you would be okay going to parallel on the stock radio. Yes, you will be able to do parallel on most all aftermarket radios. <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">   <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>If you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000021||Formula||11-06-2000||09:11 PM||||affirmitive<P>2 ohms would be too much for the factory deck to handle, actually 2 ohms is too much for anything less then a high end deck if you dont want it to overheat.  I even drew a diagram of your stereo in electronics engineering 101 today LOL.  <P>Reading back through here I dont think you ever actually said they were 4 ohm speakers.  We might have assumed it, but sometimes they come in 8 ohms too, 2 ohms is rare for dash speakers, and 16 ohms is rare for most speakers.  <P>I would also recommend rigging up your speakers before putting them in your car with speaker wire in a series to make sure you wire them right and running a multimeter in ohms on them.  (I hate blowing up amps)<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FormulaSignature2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Seanh||11-06-2000||09:44 PM||||Auctually, 8 omh speakers would have been your best bet. Sorry no one said this before you purchased them. You could have then wired them in parallel to create a 4 omh load. Just an idea. You could always use resistors (4 omh) at each speaker and then wire in parallel to create a 4 omh total load for each channel. <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">   <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>If you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000023||fropuff||11-06-2000||10:05 PM||||Actually, I installed the speakers today after school.  I built a 4x10 plate that the two speakers would fit in.  I really like the sound of four speakers in the front.  The ones I bought were Pioneer TS-G1045 2-ways.  They are 4 ohms a piece.  I wired them up like Philipine and Seanh told me to; a series circuit.  They work really good and sound good, too.  They sound a lot better than the stock 4x10s.  I reccommend this to anyone who wants to do them.  They were $140, which is a little high, but I think I got what I paid for.  Actually, they were $65 a pair originally, but were on sale for $49.95 at Best Buy.  I also got the the 4-year service plan on both sets, which ran me $40.  It was worth it to me, though.  I can go to Best Buy and get a new pair when they don't sound as good anymore.  What's next?  Well, when I get a job and have some money, I will finish my 8" sub in the floor idea, then get an amp to put in, and also a new head unit, a CD player of course.  Thanks to everyone for all the help.  I would have blown them by now if I did it with my knowledge.  Thanks again.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
